The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
dma_spec->np = of_parse_phandle(ofdma->of_node, "dma-masters", 0);
if (!dma_spec->np) {
    dev_err(&pdev->dev, "Can't get DMA master\n");
    return ERR_PTR(-EINVAL);
}
```
Reason:
The function `of_parse_phandle()` increments the reference count of the node it returns. If the node is not released using `of_node_put()` when it is no longer needed, it leads to a memory leak. In the original code, if an error occurs after this call (e.g., when `dma_spec->np` is not valid), the reference count is not decremented, causing a leak.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
of_node_put(dma_spec->np);
```
Reason:
The added `of_node_put(dma_spec->np);` statement ensures that the reference count of the node pointer is decremented when the function encounters an error and is about to return. This prevents the memory leak by properly releasing the reference to the node when it is no longer needed.

